  <!-- intro -->
  <section class="sec" id="about">
    <div class="wrap intro-grid">
      <div class="intro-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="fl">FL700</span>The aircraft</p>
        <h2>A glider with a jet engine.</h2>
        <i class="mk" data-alt="70000"></i>
      </div>
      <div class="intro-copy">
        <p class="lead">Step into the cockpit of one of the most legendary aircraft ever built. Designed in 1954 to photograph the Soviet Union from heights no fighter or missile could reach, the U-2 uncovered the missile sites that triggered the Cuban Missile Crisis, shadowed a surveillance balloon across the United States in 2023, and remains in front-line service more than seventy years after its first flight at Groom Lake. The Miltech Simulations U-2S brings this remarkable aircraft to Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 in exceptional depth, built from public data records and modelled from the wingtip skids to the edge of space.</p>
        <p>Widely regarded as the hardest aircraft in the world to fly, the U-2 rewards precision at every stage of flight. A 103 ft glider wing, a powerful GE F118 engine and bicycle landing gear combine to create a flying experience unlike any other: a climb of more than 40° nose up, a cruise between 70,000 and 80,000 ft where only a few knots separate the stall from the speed limit, and a landing flown at the very edge of the stall while a chase car races down the runway behind you, calling out your height. With no autothrottle, no antiskid and no differential brakes, every flight is yours from start to finish.</p>
        <p>At the heart of the U-2 is its mission. Whether you are painting a radar map through solid cloud, locking the SYERS-2A camera onto a distant target, or running panoramic film over a recording line, each of the three fully working sensors builds its picture live from real map data. With more than 20 sensor configurations, a complete ground support operation, four hand-crafted sceneries and seven missions included, this is the definitive high-altitude reconnaissance experience for the simulator. The U-2R and NASA’s ER-2 follow as a free update.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- history gallery -->
  <section class="history bleed" id="history">
    <div class="his-hero">
      <img width="1600" height="769" src="{{ u2base }}x_sunset.webp" loading="lazy" alt="The U-2 in silhouette against the last light at high altitude">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow"><span class="fl">FL680</span>Gallery · 1955 to today</p>
        <h2>Higher than anyone could follow.</h2>
        <i class="mk" data-alt="68000"></i>
        <p class="lede">In 1954, the United States needed photographs of the Soviet Union. No fighter and no missile could reach 70,000 feet. So the answer was an aircraft that flew above all of them. Seventy years later, it still flies.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol class="rail" id="rail" tabindex="0" aria-label="U-2 history, 1955 to 2026" style="list-style:none;margin:0">
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1955</span><span class="n">No. 01 · Nevada</span><h3>A secret first flight</h3><p>The U-2 flies for the first time at Groom Lake, a test site in the Nevada desert built for the program.</p><em>In the sim: Groom Lake, 1960s</em></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1956</span><span class="n">No. 02 · Soviet Union</span><h3>Over the Soviet Union</h3><p>The CIA starts flights over the Soviet Union. The photographs show far fewer Soviet bombers than Washington feared.</p></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1960</span><span class="n">No. 03 · Sverdlovsk</span><h3>Shot down</h3><p>On 1 May, a missile hits the U-2 of Francis Gary Powers. Two weeks later, a summit between Eisenhower and Khrushchev collapses.</p></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1962</span><span class="n">No. 04 · Cuba</span><h3>Thirteen days</h3><p>U-2 photographs find Soviet missile sites in Cuba and start the Cuban Missile Crisis. On 27 October, Maj. Rudolf Anderson is shot down over the island.</p></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1967</span><span class="n">No. 05 · U-2R</span><h3>A bigger aircraft</h3><p>A new airframe, about a third larger, with more fuel and more room for sensors.</p><em>In the sim: free update</em></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1981</span><span class="n">No. 06 · NASA</span><h3>The science version</h3><p>Production starts again as the TR-1, and NASA receives the ER-2 for high-altitude research.</p><em>In the sim: free update</em></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">1994</span><span class="n">No. 07 · U-2S</span><h3>A new engine</h3><p>The fleet gets the GE F118 turbofan, lighter and more efficient than the old J75. This is the U-2S.</p><em>In the sim: the release aircraft</em></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">2023</span><span class="n">No. 08 · United States</span><h3>The balloon</h3><p>A U-2 flies above the Chinese surveillance balloon as it crosses the United States.</p><em>In the sim: a mission</em></li>
          <li class="plq"><span class="y">2025</span><span class="n">No. 09 · Anniversary</span><h3>Seventy years</h3><p>The anniversary flight lasts 14 hours and covers 6,000 NM.</p></li>
          <li class="plq now"><span class="y">2026</span><span class="n">No. 10 · Today</span><h3>Still flying</h3><p>The U.S. Air Force still flies the U-2S from Beale AFB in California, and from forward bases such as RAF Akrotiri and RAF Fairford.</p></li>
          </ol>
      <div class="hard">
        <p class="eyebrow">Gallery · The aircraft</p>
        <h2>Why pilots call it the hardest aircraft to fly.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Think of a very long glider with a very big jet engine. Almost everything unusual about the U-2 comes from these facts.</p>
        <div class="hard-grid">
          <div class="six">
            <div><b>103 ft</b><small>A glider’s wing</small><p>Much lift and very little drag. The aircraft floats, and it does not want to slow down or come down.</p></div>
            <div><b>2 wheels</b><small>In a line</small><p>The pogos fall away on takeoff. From then on, the pilot balances the aircraft like a bicycle.</p></div>
            <div><b>½</b><small>Of the weight is fuel</small><p>The aircraft that lands is nearly a different aircraft from the one that took off.</p></div>
            <div><b>None</b><small>Automatic help</small><p>No autothrottle, no automatic spoilers, no antiskid and no differential brakes.</p></div>
          </div>
